INT. COUNTY COURTROOM - DAY

Wood-paneled, high ceilings, a flag in the corner. A jury box to one side, a witness stand, a gavel mark scarred on the bench. The gallery behind the rail is empty but for a few reporters.

JUDGE HARGROVE (60s, black robe, half-moon glasses, a tired sort of kindness) presides. ADEYEMI (40s, defense counsel, charcoal suit, calm voice) stands at the defense table beside an empty chair. LINDGREN (30s, prosecutor, sharp blazer, a stack of exhibits) sits across the aisle. BAILIFF CROWE (50s, tan uniform, mustache) guards the jury door. In the jury box, JUROR 7 (50s, plum cardigan, a clutch bag on her knee) sits very still.

HARGROVE
We'll be back on the record. Ms. Lindgren, your next witness.

LINDGREN
The State rests, Your Honor.

ADEYEMI
Your Honor, the defense moves for a directed verdict.

HARGROVE
Denied. Call your first witness, Mr. Adeyemi.

ADEYEMI
The defense calls the defendant.

A murmur in the gallery. Hargrove frowns.

HARGROVE
Counsel, your client isn't present.

ADEYEMI
He is, Your Honor. He's in the empty chair.

Lindgren half-rises.

LINDGREN
Objection. Theatrics.

ADEYEMI
The defendant is mute by medical order, Your Honor. I'm asking the court's indulgence.

HARGROVE
Approach.

Both lawyers step to the bench. Hargrove lowers her voice, but it carries.

HARGROVE (CONT'D)
Mr. Adeyemi, you will not use an empty chair as a witness.

ADEYEMI
Your Honor, my client is dead.

A beat. Lindgren stares at him.

LINDGREN
What?

ADEYEMI
He died in his cell on Sunday. I received the notice this morning.

HARGROVE
(slowly)
And you chose not to inform the court.

ADEYEMI
I'm informing the court now.

Hargrove takes off her glasses. Pinches the bridge of her nose.

HARGROVE
Bailiff Crowe. Clear the jury.

CROWE
Yes, Your Honor.

The jurors rise. Juror 7 doesn't. Crowe stops beside her.

CROWE (CONT'D)
Ma'am. Time to go.

JUROR 7
I'd like to speak.

HARGROVE
Juror, you will not.

JUROR 7
Your Honor, I think you want to hear this.

She stands. She opens her clutch bag. From inside she draws a folded sheet of paper and a small silver badge.

JUROR 7 (CONT'D)
I'm Special Agent Delacroix, Your Honor. I've been in that box for nine days.

The room goes dead quiet. Lindgren drops her pen. Adeyemi closes his eyes.

DELACROIX
The defendant didn't die of natural causes. The man he's accused of killing is alive. And somebody in this courtroom has been feeding him information since Monday.

HARGROVE
Agent Delacroix, you are in contempt.

DELACROIX
I know.

HARGROVE
Do you have proof?

DELACROIX
Yes, Your Honor.

She hands the paper to Crowe, who carries it to the bench. Hargrove reads it. Her face changes.

HARGROVE
Ms. Lindgren.

LINDGREN
Your Honor?

HARGROVE
Your signature is on this.

LINDGREN
(very quietly)
I want a lawyer.

ADEYEMI
Funny. So did my client.

Crowe moves toward Lindgren. Lindgren grabs her exhibits, shoves them at Adeyemi, and bolts for the side door. Delacroix is faster. She crosses the well in three strides and catches Lindgren's wrist at the door.

DELACROIX
Counselor, don't make this harder.

LINDGREN
Let me go!

Hargrove brings down the gavel. Once. Twice. Three times.

HARGROVE
This court is adjourned.

She sets the gavel down. Her hand shakes. In the sudden silence, a reporter's pencil snaps.

ADEYEMI
(sotto, to himself)
Finally.
